(Tangkhul Roman Alphabet & Vowel System) Tj
0 -11 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(Tangkhul utilizes a Latin-based phonetic alphabet introduced by Rev. William Pettigrew in 1897 and standardized by the Tangkhul Literature Society \(TLS\). Vowels: a, ?, e, i, o, u \(short and long variants\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(Linguistic Rules & Examples:) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Long vowels marked with macrons \(?, ?, ?, ?, ?\) distinguish lexical minimal pairs \(e.g., 'ram' [country] vs 'r?m' [dry field]\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Diphthongs include: ei \(meira\), ai \(kakhai\), ao \(vanao\), ui \(ngashui\), oi \(choi\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 7 Tf
( ) Tj
0 -9 Td
/F2 9 Tf
(Consonant Inventory) Tj
0 -11 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(Stops: p, t, k, ph, th, kh, b, d, g. Nasals: m, n, ng. Fricatives: s, sh, h, z. Liquids: r, l. Glides: w, y.) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(Linguistic Rules & Examples:) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Velar nasal 'ng' can occur in syllable initial, medial, and coda positions \(e.g., ngalei [earth], thingrong [tree]\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Initial consonant clusters predominantly involve liquids: pr-, tr-, kr-, phr-, thr-, khr-.) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 7 Tf
( ) Tj
0 -9 Td
