(Core Case Postpositions) Tj
0 -11 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(Tangkhul is an agglutinative postpositional language. Cases are attached directly to the noun phrase head.) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(Linguistic Rules & Examples:) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Nominative / Agentive: -na \(e.g., I-na shansera -> I have read\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Accusative / Dative: -li \(e.g., Ali milu -> Give to him; Vanaoli thei -> Saw the bird\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Instrumental: -eina \(e.g., Ngaphei-eina tulu -> Cut with a machete\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Genitive / Possessive: -wui \(e.g., Shim-wui kuiphung -> The pillar of the house\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Locative: -li \(e.g., Kaphung-li pamda -> Residing on the mountain\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Ablative: -wui eina / -li eina \(e.g., Ukhrul-wui eina kazat -> Coming from Ukhrul\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Comitative / Associative: -le / -nga \(e.g., Amei-le kazat -> Walking together with brother\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 7 Tf
( ) Tj
0 -9 Td
/F1 8 Tf
