(Adjective Formation & Comparison) Tj
0 -11 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(Most Tangkhul adjectives are derived from stative verbal roots prefixed with ka- or kha-.) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(Linguistic Rules & Examples:) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Positive: kapha \(good\), kakhangar \(heavy\), kathuk \(deep\), kahung \(red\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Comparative suffix: -shang / -meila \(kapha-meila -> better than\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Superlative suffix: -saikora wui meila / -tak \(kaphatak -> the very best\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 7 Tf
( ) Tj
0 -9 Td
/F2 9 Tf
(Cardinal Numerals) Tj
0 -11 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(1: akha, 2: khani, 3: kathum, 4: mati, 5: phanga, 6: taruk, 7: shini, 8: shet, 9: chiko, 10: thara, 20: maku, 100: sha akha.) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(Linguistic Rules & Examples:) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 8 Tf
(  * Numerals follow the noun they qualify: mi kathum \(three persons\), thingrong mati \(four trees\).) Tj
0 -10 Td
/F1 7 Tf
